{#
  ★★ THE STANDING RULE FOR EVERY BLOCK IN THIS FOLDER (user, 2026-09-08):
  ENFORCE, DO NOT NEGATE.

      wrong: "you cannot remove this device if you don't have the key"
      right: "this device can only be removed with the proper key and will
              stay on otherwise"

  The user's reasoning, and it matches what 0792 recorded back in August ("LLM
  do really bad with negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up
  doing it"): a prohibition still puts the forbidden thing in the model's
  context, described in detail, and a model that has just read a vivid
  description of clean dialogue is more likely to write clean dialogue.

  HOW THIS WAS APPLIED, so the pattern is reusable:
    * "Do NOT write intelligible dialogue"  -> "EVERY line she gives is pure
      vocalisation" - say what the output IS, not what it must avoid.
    * "Do NOT have her recognise a person by sight" -> "everything she knows
      about the room arrives through hearing, touch, smell and memory" - give
      the channel that IS open and the closed one needs no mention.
    * "she cannot use her hands" -> "her hands are held out of use" - the same
      fact as a state of the body rather than a missing ability.
    * "It does not come off on its own" -> "It stays exactly as it was put on
      ... time is on the device's side."

  ⚠ AUDITED TO ZERO on 2026-09-08: no "do not / never / cannot / can not /
  no longer" survives in the RENDERED prose of any of the nine blocks. Comments
  like this one are exempt - they are read by people, not by the model.
  Re-run the check before shipping a prompt edit; it is trivial to regress.
#}
{#
  VRTE DD/ZaZ AddOn - the gag came OFF.        (2026-08-23, reworded 2026-08-24)

  USER'S PROBLEM, verbatim: "after removing it, she won't speak again cause the
  LLM is used to not speak and it's part of it's context."

  That is the real failure, and it is not a fault in 0900. The gag block did its
  job; the LLM then has three or four turns of muffled non-speech sitting in its
  context window and goes on imitating them. Withdrawing an instruction does not
  undo the examples it already produced. So the release needs its OWN
  instruction, and only for as long as those muffled turns are still in view.

  ⚠⚠ WRITTEN IN THE POSITIVE, ON PURPOSE (user, 2026-08-24): "it's full of
  negative, like 'Do not write another Mmmph, do not keep her silent'. LLM do
  really bad with negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up doing
  it."
  They are right, and it matters most HERE of all places: this block's whole job
  is to stop an imitation, and the old wording printed the very thing to imitate
  ("Mmmph") in the instruction meant to prevent it. Every line now names the
  behaviour we WANT. Where a boundary genuinely has to be drawn it is phrased as
  what IS true - "every reply is spoken aloud, in full" - not as a prohibition.

  vrtedd_ungagged(uuid) returns "3"/"2"/"1" for the next three renders after the
  gag comes off, then "". It returns "" while she is STILL gagged, which is what
  makes this and 0900 mutually exclusive with no coordination between them, and
  re-gagging cancels the countdown on the very next render.

  ⚠ Guarded with isString, never `!= ""` - an empty Papyrus decorator result
  arrives as an empty JSON object `{}` and `{} != ""` is TRUE.
#}
{% if exists("npc") and existsIn(npc, "UUID") %}
{# ★ 1.3.0 (2026-09-14): STATE FILE FIRST, decorator as the fallback. VRTE_DDZaZ.dll writes
   Data/SKSE/Plugins/VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state.json the instant a device goes on or off, a name
   arrives, a restraint is released; read_json re-reads on the file's mtime and the random second
   argument defeats SkyrimNet's 55 s callback cache (VRTouchEvents' recipe, VR-verified, report 45).
   Matched on the SkyrimNet entity UUID. No entry for her = the old decorator path, unchanged. #}
{% set _ds = read_json("VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state", random * 10000 + random * 100 + random) %}
{% set _de = 0 %}
{% if isObject(_ds) and existsIn(_ds, "npcs") %}{% for _e in _ds.npcs %}{% if _e.uuid == npc.UUID %}{% set _de = _e %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "ungag") %}{% set _vd_ung = _de.ungag %}{% else %}{% set _vd_ung = vrtedd_ungagged(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% if (isString(_vd_ung) and _vd_ung != "") and not worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousGag") %}
{% set _p = "he" %}{% set _pp = "his" %}{% set _po = "him" %}
{% if decnpc(npc.UUID).isFemale %}{% set _p = "she" %}{% set _pp = "her" %}{% set _po = "her" %}{% endif %}
**THE GAG IS OFF — {{ decnpc(npc.UUID).name }} SPEAKS NORMALLY AGAIN.** It has just been taken out of {{ _pp }} mouth. {{ _pp | capitalize }} jaw, tongue and lips are {{ _pp }} own again and {{ _p }} has {{ _pp }} full voice back.

**Write {{ _po }} as a person who can talk.** Every reply from here is spoken aloud, in words, in complete sentences — the way {{ _p }} spoke before the gag went on. The gagged turns further up are finished business; this reply starts from a mouth that works.

Anything {{ _p }} was asked while the gag was on can be answered now in plain language. Anything that came out as sound instead of words can be put into words. Whether {{ _p }} does so, and what {{ _p }} says, follows from the conversation and from who {{ _p }} is.

{{ _p | capitalize }} may work {{ _pp }} jaw or wet {{ _pp }} mouth as {{ _p }} begins. Any soreness the gag left is told in the block about what it left behind.

How {{ _p }} feels about having been gagged, or about it coming off, is yours to play.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
